  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nWhile slower than previous model guidance suggested, shallow \nfog is beginning to expand in coverage early this morning across\nportions of the area outside of Chicago, some of which is \ndense. The general expectation is for areas of fog to persist \nthrough the first part of the morning commute, with some fog \npotentially drifting into portions of Chicago toward daybreak \n(though confidence in this remains on the lower side). Any fog \nthat does develop should erode quickly between 8-10 AM CDT.\n\nGOES water vapor imagery depicts the high-amplitude upper level\npattern ongoing across the CONUS early this morning. Narrow \nridging located across the Mississippi Valley and western Great \nLakes is squeezed in between deep upper troughing and associated\nclosed low centered over southern CA/NV, and the remnants of \nthe slow moving Nor'easter currently drifting northeast across \nNew England.\n\nIn between the western trough and the upper ridge overhead \nthere is an expansive plume of deep moisture lifting across the \nGreat Plains toward the Upper Mississippi Valley, which is \nexpected to be further moistened with the arrival of the \ntropical remnants of Polo which recently made landfall across  \ncentral Baja California. This will set the stage for a multi- \nday stretch of rainfall across the region, beginning potentially\nas early as tonight here locally into portions of northwest \nIllinois as warm advective showers blossom atop a low-level \nfrontal zone. Shower coverage then gradually expands across much\nof the area on Wednesday, including the Chicago metro. \nConfidence in the timing of the various rounds of greater shower\ncoverage remain on the lower side, with some breaks possible at\ntimes.\n\nThe combination of both Gulf and Pacific moisture will feed \nanomalously high moisture content into the region. In fact, \nmodeled PWATs approaching or even exceeding 2\" is toward the \nupper echelon of climatology per DVN/ILX RAOBs for this time of\nyear. This would support the potential for heavy rainfall and \nan associated localized flooding threat. Do think this may be \nmainly tied to any isolated embedded thunderstorms that \npotentially redevelop over a narrow more focused corridor, \nthough confidence in if and where that occurs remains low. The \naforementioned closed upper low across the SW CONUS will be slow\nto merge back into the northern stream upper jet, which may \nlead to showers persisting into the day on Friday as well before\nthe axis shifts fully south of the area Friday evening.\n\nTemperatures through late week will be dependent upon shower \ncoverage each day with temperatures in the lower to mid 70s\nfavored in areas with greater shower coverage and upper 70s to \naround 80 in areas south of the rainfall axis (likely south of \nI-80).\n\nThe upper levels then transition toward a zonal to quasi-zonal \npattern for the weekend into early next week. While a few low-\namplitude waves likely move within the flow aloft, any \nassociated precipitation is currently favored to remain mainly \nnorth of the area. North to northeasterly surface winds will \nsupport cooler temperatures to start the first full week of \nOctober with highs in the 60s and lows in the 40s to around 50.\n\nPetr",
